+# Trip builder: AI chat ("Chat to change it")
+
+Travellers can edit their trip on the screen (remove, add, swap, reorder, move tours between days, undo) and chat to an AI that makes the same edits for them. The screen editing needs nothing from the backend. The chat needs one Edge Function, one migration and an Anthropic API key. Until the key is set the builder quietly uses its built-in quick helper ("remove Hoi An", "add Bangkok for 3 nights"), so nothing breaks and the order you do things in doesn't matter.
+
+## What's in this change
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| `public/trip-builder/` | the builder with editing + chat (`edit.js`, `chat.js`, `ui-chat.js`, `app.js`, `logic.js`, `styles.css`, `config.js`) |
+| `supabase/functions/trip-builder-chat/` | the Edge Function, plus `chat-spec.json` (the system prompt and tools; copied from the builder repo by its sync script, don't edit here) |
+| `supabase/functions/_shared/trip-builder-chat.ts` (+ test) | the checks on what the browser sends and the request made to Claude |
+| `supabase/migrations/20261022090000_trip_builder_chat_usage.sql` | daily counters so cost can't run away |
+| `supabase/config.toml` | `verify_jwt = false` for the function (like the other trip-builder ones) |
+
+## Switching it on
+
+1. Apply the migration (Lovable, like the others).
+2. Add the secret `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` (Anthropic Console > API keys). **The bill goes to whoever owns that key.**
+3. Deploy `trip-builder-chat`.
+4. Try it on the live trip builder: open a trip, tap **Chat to change it**, and send a few messages. The header should say "AI assistant". If it says "Quick helper" the function answered 503 (no key, or the migration isn't applied: check the function logs).
+
+Nothing in this change has talked to the real Claude API; it was tested against a fake one (below). The first few real conversations are the real test.
+
+## Settings (all optional secrets)
+
+| Secret | Default | |
+|---|---|---|
+| `TRIP_CHAT_MODEL` | `claude-opus-5-5` | `claude-sonnet-5-5` is half the price, `claude-haiku-5-5` a fraction of that |
+| `TRIP_CHAT_EFFORT` | `medium` | `low` / `medium` / `high`: how hard it thinks (speed and cost) |
+| `TRIP_CHAT_MAX_TOKENS` | `6000` | per call; thinking counts towards it |
+| `TRIP_CHAT_IP_DAILY` | `60` | calls per person per day (a chat message is 1 to 4 calls) |
+| `TRIP_CHAT_DAILY` | `1500` | calls per day across everyone |
+| `TRIP_CHAT_ORIGINS` | any | e.g. `https://madmonkeyhostels.com` to refuse other sites' browsers |
+| `TRIP_CHAT_ENABLED` | on | `false` switches the AI off (the quick helper takes over) |
+
+## Cost
+
+Opus 5.5 is $4 in / $20 out per million tokens; Sonnet 5.5 is $2 / $10; Haiku 5.5 is $0.10 / $0.50. By the size of the prompts a chat message should cost roughly 5 to 15 cents on Opus, 2 to 8 on Sonnet and well under a cent on Haiku. That is an estimate, not measured. Each call writes one log line (`{"trip_chat":1,"model":...,"input_tokens":...,"output_tokens":...,"cache_read_input_tokens":...}`) so the real number is easy to work out after a few days. The daily limits above are the ceiling: at the defaults, the worst case is 1500 calls a day. Keep an eye on it for the first week.
+
+## What it does and doesn't do
+
+- The browser sends only the conversation. The system prompt, tools, model and limits are set here, so the URL can't be used as a general Claude.
+- The tool calls (look up places, tours and travel; edit the trip) run in the traveller's browser on their own trip. This function stores nothing and has no access to bookings, requests or payments.
+- Every request is checked (shape, sizes, block types, tool names), counted against the limits **before** Claude is called, and refused with 429 once a limit is reached. If the counter itself fails it refuses (503) rather than spend blind.
+- People are counted by a salted hash of their IP; message text is never logged.
+- Claude's own error messages are never passed on to the browser.
+
+## Checks
+
+- `deno test supabase/functions/_shared/trip-builder-chat.test.ts`
+- `handoff/all-in-backend/e2e/run.sh <this repo>` (in the trip builder repo) runs the real builder code against this function against a fake Claude on localhost. 53 payment checks and 37 chat checks.
